# Coach speech (Turkish): the appraisal after the game (src/coach/appraisal.cpp).
# Same keys and variants as assets/coach/speech/en/appraisal.lang (its header documents every placeholder).
# The appraisal is always positive and never blames. The coach says "sen" and calls himself "ben";
# Turkish has no grammatical gender, nothing agrees with the coach or the player.
#
# No suffix is ever written after a placeholder: it goes on a fixed noun next to it.
#   Move numbers: {n:ord} + a noun ("{n:ord} hamlede" = "21. hamlede", spoken "yirmi birinci hamlede";
#   "{crit_no:ord} hamleye kadar"; "{crit_no:ord} hamleydi"). Counts end in their noun, which may take
#   the suffix: "{moves:move}de" (24 hamlede), "{pts:point}lık materyal" (3 puanlık), "{n:move}den".
#   Moves stand after a colon, before "yerine", or before "hamlesi" / "hamlesiyle" (a spoken capture
#   ends in the verb "alır", so a move is never the bare subject of a verb).
#   Accuracy: {acc:percent} = "%85", spoken "yüzde seksen beş" ("{acc:percent} doğrulukla").
# Names inserted by {text}, {theme}, {level}:
#   appraisal.phase.*   bare nouns, exactly "açılış", "oyun ortası", "oyun sonu": common.lang gives their
#                       cases ("{text:loc} çok iyi oynadın", "{text:acc} çok iyi oynadın").
#   appraisal.reason.*  an adverbial said before "berabere" ("Pozisyon tekrarı nedeniyle berabere.").
#   theme.*             an infinitive phrase in lower case, no full stop, said after a colon.
#   appraisal.level.*   the coach page's level name (coach.level.<n>.name) in quotation marks, always
#                       before "seviye" with its case ending: "{level} seviyesine hazırsın".
# {opening} is the opening's name in the nominative: subject, predicate, after a colon, or before "ile".

